<!--
  /deck — Deck overview. The shell sets data-theme="deck" (violet); this page
  is pure content. Adapted from packages/deck/docs/routes/page.sui to the
  sui-site's .doc prose convention (eyebrow / h1 / lead / h2).
-->
<article class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">deck · 0.2.0</p>
  <h1>The standalone web framework for SUI.</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    deck mounts SUI views, routes them from the filesystem, server-renders or
    statically builds them, and serves every asset — with a dev server and
    state-preserving HMR. It is to SUI what Next is to React.
  </p>

  <alert class="callout">
    <strong>This site is a deck app.</strong> Every page you read is a file-routed
    deck route, server-rendered and hydrated. The docs are deck's own canonical
    example — and its release test.
  </alert>

  <h2>The 30-second app</h2>
  <p>A deck app is a <code>routes/</code> folder and a dev server. No config file, no scaffold step.</p>
  <pre class="code">{quickstart}</pre>
  <p>That's a running SUI single-page app, with HMR, on <code>127.0.0.1:7878</code>.</p>

  <h2>Where it sits</h2>
  <pre class="code">{layers}</pre>
  <p>
    SUI is the component language — signals, templates, controllers. deck is the
    framework around it — routing, rendering, build, serving. Your app is what
    you write under <code>routes/</code>.
  </p>

  <row class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/deck/getting-started">Getting started →</a>
  </row>
</article>
